The essay’s central distinction is between:

- **content disagreement**, which can occur within a shared evidentiary world;
- **semantic-environment collapse**, in which the shared world itself can no longer be reliably pointed to.

The disappearing island contains:

- stable sources;
- persistent links;
- named authorship;
- preserved context;
- verifiable chains;
- terms whose meaning does not change mid-dispute.

The essay connects this infrastructure to:

- democratic deliberation;
- labor organizing;
- climate response;
- resistance to Big-Lie repetition;
- preservation of community memory.

Its proposed counter-practice is a restored **citational commons**.
